        "testata": "Al Jazeera",
        "lingua_originale": "en",
        "titolo_orig": "LIVE: UK PM Keir Starmer expected to announce his resignation",
        "testo_it": "Il premier britannico Keir Starmer è vicino ad annunciare le dimissioni, aprendo una crisi politica nel Regno Unito.",
        "priorita": "Altissima",
        "id_gruppo": 12,
        "link": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/6/22/live-uk-pm-keir-starmer-expected-to-announce-his-resignation?traffic_source=rss",
        "orario": "09:39",
        "timestamp": 1782113949,
        "contenuto_esteso": "4 Updates live-orange 1m ago ( 08:00 GMT ) What happens if Keir Starmer resigns without a fight, and what comes next? By Jonah Hull Bear in mind that this sort of thing is not in the DNA of the Labour Party and its MPs. They have never been opposed to a sitting prime minister. There are many MPs deeply uncomfortable with the process. It could go one of two ways: either resulting in a leadership contest where viable candidates go up against the prime minister himself, put forward their policies for scrutiny and are judged by the members of the party, or, as some are concerned about, frankly, a coronation of Andy Burnham after he becomes an MP this afternoon. He has a substantial and enormous level of support, by all accounts. I think there are two imperatives for the party. One is a smooth, orderly transition that has some continuity attached and does not roil the financial markets. The other is for the party and members to get some look at Andy Burnham’s actual policy platform. live-orange 11m ago ( 07:50 GMT ) Keir Starmer expected to address future after painful weekend By Jonah Hull It will have been a painful weekend of self-reflection for Keir Starmer. There is now a high expectation that in the coming hours, perhaps indeed in the very next hour, the prime minister will emerge from Number 10 Downing Street, where a podium will be brought out for him, and he will make a statement about his future. Number 10 has not confirmed that he is going to resign. The likelihood, though, based on plenty of briefing over the weekend, is that he will set out some sort of timetable for his departure and for an orderly transition, as he has been urged to do by senior cabinet ministers. And Keir Starmer has been holed up all weekend at Chequers, his country residence, talking to people, listening, thinking and reflecting on his position, particularly on his determination – repeated only as recently as Friday – to fight any challenge against him and to defend his government’s majority won less than two years ago. What has happened since? Well, months of bad decisions, self-inflicted mistakes, painful and embarrassing turnarounds and U-turns, and local elections a month or so ago that were disastrous for him and for Labour. live-orange 16m ago ( 07:45 GMT ) WATCH: Starmer faces growing pressure as Labour unrest deepens after by-election Starmer is facing mounting pressure from MPs and ministers of his own Labour Party to step down. And the win last week by former Manchester Mayor Andy Burnham in a critical United Kingdom by-election has added further challenges to Starmer’s leadership. Watch the report below: Advertisement live-orange 21m ago ( 07:39 GMT ) Welcome to our live coverage Hello and thank you for joining our live coverage of the latest developments in British politics. Beleaguered Prime Minister Keir Starmer is expected to deliver a statement amid media reports he plans to resign. Stay with us as we bring you all the latest developments, reactions and analyses throughout the day."
